---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { article, breadcrumb } from '../lib/seo';
const schema = [
  article({ site:Astro.site, path:'/playbook', headline:'How Ground Floor tests local AI', description:'The public evidence method for local-AI field tests.', techArticle:true }),
  breadcrumb(Astro.site, [{ name:'Ground Floor', path:'/' }, { name:'How testing works', path:'/playbook' }]),
];
---
<Base title="How Ground Floor tests local AI" description="The public evidence method for local-AI field tests." appearance="light" schema={schema}>
  <header class="gf-page-hero"><div class="gf-shell"><p class="gf-eyebrow">Public testing method</p><h1 class="gf-display mt-4">How does a local-AI claim earn trust?</h1><p class="gf-lede">Another person must be able to inspect the configuration, safe inputs, raw outputs, rubric, timing, and failures that produced it.</p></div></header>
  <div class="gf-content"><div class="gf-shell"><article class="gf-prose">
    <h2>What evidence labels does Ground Floor use?</h2>
    <p><strong>Measured</strong> means a completed run has retained configuration, fixture, prompt, raw output, timing, errors, and evaluation. <strong>Estimate</strong> is a calculation from stated assumptions. <strong>Third-party</strong> is attributed external evidence. <strong>Protocol</strong> is a test plan without a completed result.</p>
    <h2>What makes a task testable?</h2>
    <ol><li>It happens often enough to matter.</li><li>The source and required output are known.</li><li>A qualified reviewer can define and score correctness.</li><li>The test can use synthetic, public, or properly sanitized material.</li><li>A human owns the final work and decision.</li></ol>
    <h2>What must a measured run retain?</h2>
    <ul><li>Machine, memory, operating system, runtime, and settings.</li><li>Model identity, source, quantization, and artifact hash when available.</li><li>Exact prompts and safe test fixtures.</li><li>Every raw output, including failed examples.</li><li>Predeclared rubric, reviewer role, corrections, and critical errors.</li><li>Timing method, repetitions, context size, and variance.</li></ul>
    <h2>When is a result useful?</h2>
    <p>The pass and failure thresholds are fixed before the run. A result is useful only inside the task, hardware, model, fixtures, and review process actually tested. A few attractive examples are not a result.</p>
    <h2>What does the method not answer?</h2>
    <p>Ground Floor does not provide legal, clinical, tax, investment, accounting, privacy, security, or compliance approval. It measures a technical workflow. Qualified professionals decide whether that workflow belongs in a real practice.</p>
    <p><a class="gf-link" href="/experiments">Inspect the current protocols</a></p>
  </article></div></div>
</Base>
